<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANONYME_-_Paradoxographus_Vaticanus/paradoxographus_vaticanus_zoological_excerpts/e>
      a       zoo:Oeuvre ;
      zoo:author "ANONYME - Paradoxographus Vaticanus" ;
      zoo:date "2026-08-20" ;
      zoo:editor "Unknown editor" ;
      zoo:file "./zoo/zoo86/1e.xml" ;
      zoo:identifier "paradoxographus_vaticanus_zoological_excerpts" ;
      zoo:title "Paradoxographus Vaticanus zoological excerpts" .


<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANONYME_-_Paradoxographus_Vaticanus/paradoxographus_vaticanus_zoological_excerpts/e/2/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANONYME_-_Paradoxographus_Vaticanus/paradoxographus_vaticanus_zoological_excerpts/e/2> ;
      zoo:text "3 Polites says that the young tuna (pelamys) in the Euxine Sea are generated from mud (pelos), and he says that they get their name because of this." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANONYME_-_Paradoxographus_Vaticanus/paradoxographus_vaticanus_zoological_excerpts/e/1/6/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANONYME_-_Paradoxographus_Vaticanus/paradoxographus_vaticanus_zoological_excerpts/e/1/6> ;
      zoo:text "7(8)[1] Snakes have thirty ribs. [2] And if one pricks their eyes out, they grow back again, just like those of the swallow." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANONYME_-_Paradoxographus_Vaticanus/paradoxographus_vaticanus_zoological_excerpts/e/1/1/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANONYME_-_Paradoxographus_Vaticanus/paradoxographus_vaticanus_zoological_excerpts/e/1/1> ;
      zoo:text "1[1] Hagesias of Megara says that the cranes, whenever they are about to fly away from Thrace, are all sprinkled in purification in a circle by one of their number: then, when that one cries out, they rise up from the ground as if at the command of a boatswain, while the one that purifies them remains there. [2] Whenever they fly over the sea, two of them stretch out their wings, while one that has become tired rests by settling on them." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANONYME_-_Paradoxographus_Vaticanus/paradoxographus_vaticanus_zoological_excerpts/e/1/3/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANONYME_-_Paradoxographus_Vaticanus/paradoxographus_vaticanus_zoological_excerpts/e/1/3> ;
      zoo:text "4[1] Aristotle says in his works on animals that all land animals that have lungs breathe, but that the wasp and bee do not breathe. [2](5) Those that have bladders also have intestines, but not all that have intestines have bladders." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANONYME_-_Paradoxographus_Vaticanus/paradoxographus_vaticanus_zoological_excerpts/e/1/8/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANONYME_-_Paradoxographus_Vaticanus/paradoxographus_vaticanus_zoological_excerpts/e/1/8> ;
      zoo:text "9(10)[1] Polycleitus says that tortoises are found even in the Ganges, whose shells can hold five medimnoi. [2] Agatharchides [says] that they use †many† shells to roof their huts." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANONYME_-_Paradoxographus_Vaticanus/paradoxographus_vaticanus_zoological_excerpts/e/1/4/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANONYME_-_Paradoxographus_Vaticanus/paradoxographus_vaticanus_zoological_excerpts/e/1/4> ;
      zoo:text "5(6) Many animals are bloodless, but in general those that have more than four feet." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANONYME_-_Paradoxographus_Vaticanus/paradoxographus_vaticanus_zoological_excerpts/e/1/7/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANONYME_-_Paradoxographus_Vaticanus/paradoxographus_vaticanus_zoological_excerpts/e/1/7> ;
      zoo:text "8(9) The bones of lions are so hard that when struck they often kindle fire." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANONYME_-_Paradoxographus_Vaticanus/paradoxographus_vaticanus_zoological_excerpts/e/1/2/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANONYME_-_Paradoxographus_Vaticanus/paradoxographus_vaticanus_zoological_excerpts/e/1/2> ;
      zoo:text "2 Dalion says, in Book 1 of his Aithiopika, that there is a beast in Ethiopia called the krokotta; when it approaches farmsteads, it eavesdrops upon those who are chatting, in particular on the names of the children. Coming by night, it speaks the names and, when the children come out, they are devoured by it." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANONYME_-_Paradoxographus_Vaticanus/paradoxographus_vaticanus_zoological_excerpts/e/1/5/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/ANONYME_-_Paradoxographus_Vaticanus/paradoxographus_vaticanus_zoological_excerpts/e/1/5> ;
      zoo:text "6(7) Fish do not have a gullet: because of this, the stomach of larger fish falls forward into their mouths when they chase smaller ones." .


